  "raw_text": "259\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nlff-29\n):\n \nUpla kum aiska pali yapuya taim, ai tnaya kir ra dîa taki ba nu âpu sa.\n \n–\n \nWhen a person is really\nsleeping (deeply), they are not aware of what is happening around them.\n \n/ Cuando una persona está bien\ndormida (durmiendo profundamente), no se da cuenta de lo que sucede a su alrededor.)\n\nWan tnaya kir\n \n–\n \nall around us\n \n/ a nuestro alrededor\n\nTnaya kutbanka\n \n(a/t:\n \nNaya kutbanka\n,\n \nSnaya kutbanka\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \nwall\n \n/ pared\n\nTaun tnaya kutbanka\n \n(a/t:\n \nTaun naya kutbanka\n,\n \nTaun snaya kutbanka\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \ncity wall\n \n/ muralla de la\ncuidad\n\nTnaya mina\n \n(a/t:\n \nNaya mina\n,\n \nSnaya mina\n)\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nrib, spine\n \n/ costilla, columna vertebral\n\nTnaya\n \nra\n \n(a/t:\n \nTnayara, N\naya\n \nra\n,\n \nNaya ra\n,\n \nSnaya\n \nra\n,\n \nSnayara\n)\n \n(adv)\n \n–\n \nbeside\n, to the side\n \n/ al lado\n\nTnaya ra lakaia\n \n(a/t:\n \nTnayara lakaia, Nayara lakaia\n,\n \nSnayara lakaia\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto turn to the side, to turn aside,\nto turn away, to separate\n \n/ girar hacia un lado, alejarse, separar\n\nTnaya ra swiaia\n \n(a/t:\n \nTnayara swiaia, Nayara swiaia\n,\n \nSnayara swiaia\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto leave on the side, to leave\nto the side, to allow to go to the side\n \n/ dejar de lado, dejar ir de lado\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nwqu-07/20\n):\n \nPal dîa luki kan dîa dîa ai tnayara swin ba dukiara?\n \n–\n \nWhat was Paul thinking about everything\nthat he left to the side (gave up)?\n \n/ ¿Qué pensaba Pablo acerca de todo lo que dejó a un lado (renunció)?)\n\nTnaya ra tawaia\n \n(a/t:\n \nTnayara tawaia, Tnayara tauaia\n,\n \nNayara tawaia\n,\n \nNayara tauaia\n,\n \nSnayara tawaia\n,\n\nSnayara tauaia\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto turn to the side, to turn aside, to turn away, to stray, to stray from the good\n \n/\nvolverse a un lado, apartarse, alejarse, alejarse del bien\n\nTnikaia\n \n(v-trans; intrans:\n \nTniwaia\n)\n \n–\n \nto lay down, to lean, to incline\n \n/ acostarse, inclinarse\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nlfb70\n):\n \nMeri kwala wal blaki tânkira daiwan pata watla kum ra tnikan\n \n–\n \nMary gently wrapped (him) with cloth and laid (him)\ndown in a place for animal food (manger)\n \n/ María lo envolvió gentilmente con tela y lo acostó en un lugar para comida de\nanimales (pesebre))\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nhf-7\n):\n \nkiama pri tniki wals\n \n–\n \ngive undivided attention\n \n/ prestar toda la atención\n \n(Lit:\n \nlisten (with your) ear free and leaning\n \n/\nescucha (con tu) oreja libre e inclinada)\n\nTniwaia\n \n(v-intrans; trans:\n \nTnikaia\n)\n \n–\n \nto lean, to incline, to be leaning\n \n/ apoyarse, inclinarse\n\nLuki tniwaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto trust, to rely, to depend on\n \n/ confiar, contar con, depender de\n\n(Note/Nota: It is more common to use\n \nKasak lukaia\n \nto say:\n \nto believe, to have faith, to have confidence\n \n/ Es más común\nusar\n \nKasak lukaia\n \npara decir “creer, tener fe, tener confianza, confiar”\n)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nwqu-07/20\n):\n \nYawan silp wan karnika ra tniwi lukbiara\n \n–\n \nWe are not to trust in our own strength\n \n/ No debemos\nconfiar en nuestro propia fuerza)\n\n(Ex/Ej:\n \nYawan silp wan lukanka ra tniwi lukbiara\n \n–\n \nWe are not to trust in our own thinking\n \n/ No debemos confiar en\nnuestro propio pensamiento)\n\nTrabil\n \n(a/t:\n \nTrabel\n,\n \nTrobel\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \ntrouble, problem, distress\n \n/ problema, angustia\n\n(Construct:\n \nTrabilki\n \n–\n \nmy trouble/problem\n \n/ mi problema;\n \nTrabilkam\n \n–\n \nyour trouble/problem\n \n/ tu problema;\n \nTrabilka\n \n–\n \nhis/her\ntrouble/problem\n \n/ su problema)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nll-2\n):\n \nhilp mai munbia trabilkam nani pura luaia\n \n–\n \nwill help you overcome your problems\n \n/ te ayudará a superar tus\nproblemas)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nmwb-08/19\n):\n \nTasba ra plun wahwanka trabilka ba nahki wapni takbia ki?\n \n–\n \nHow will the problem of world hunger come to\nbe solved?\n \n/ ¿Cómo se resolverá el problema del hambre mundial?)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nmwb-09/19\n):\n \nYawan nahki nu sa Gâd ba wan trabilka nani tânka brisa ki?\n \n–\n \nHow do we know that God understands our\nproblems?\n \n/ ¿Cómo sabemos que Dios entiende nuestros problemas?)\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nhf\n-\n9\n):\n \nbîla kaikras\n \ntrabilka kum takaia sip sa\n \n–\n \nan unexpected problem could occur\n \n/ podría ocurrir un problema inesperado\n)\n\nTrabil âpu\n \n–\n \nno trouble\n, no problem\n \n/ no hay problema\n\nTrabil briaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto have a problem, to have troubles, to have problems\n \n/ tener un problema, tener problemas\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nhf27\n):\n \nKupiam krauks, man trabil brisma ba Gȃd wina apia sa\n \n–\n \nDon’t forget, your troubles are not from God\n \n/ No lo\nolvides, tus problemas no son de Dios\n)\n\nTrabil munaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto trouble, to bother, to annoy, to cause distress, to cause anxiety\n \n/ molestar, causar angustia,\ncausar ansiedad\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nhf-14\n):\n \nLalah briaia ba diara tara sa, sakuna maritkam lâka ra yarka saura munbiara bara uba trabil mai munbiara\n\n–\n \nIt is important to have money. However, you are not to allow it to harm your marriage nor to cause you anxiety.\n \n/ Es\nimportante tener dinero.\n \nSin embargo, no debes permitirle dañar tu matrimonio ni causarte ansiedad\n.\n)\n\nTrabil piua\n \n–\n \ntime of trouble\n \n/ tiempo de problemas\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nwqu\n-\n11/19\n):\n \nYawan nahki nu sa Jirimaya ba pâna pain kan trabil\n \npiua ra?\n \n–\n \nHow do we that\n \nJeremiah was a good\n\nfriend in a time of distress?\n \n/ ¿Cómo sabemos que Jeremías era un buen amigo en tiempos de angustia?\n)",
